<h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Set up SparrowKit</h1>
<p class="mt-1 text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  SparrowKit is a set of Rails engines that handle the parts of an application
  that are the same every time — signing in, sending mail, taking money — so
  that the part that is not the same every time is the part you write.
</p>


<%#
  Asked once, here, because more than one panel needs it in a different shape:
  Authentication wants the bare host that passkeys bind to, Payments wants the
  whole address a processor returns a customer to. Two boxes for one fact is
  one typo away from passkeys bound to a domain nobody visits.

  It DEFAULTS those boxes; it does not replace them. Each module keeps its own
  copy under its own key, because sparrow_ui is a development-group gem and is
  not installed in production -- a value that lived only here would vanish on
  deploy.
%>
<section class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
  <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Your application</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
    Where this application lives on the web, and what it is called. Every module
    that needs either starts from here, so both are typed once and only once.
  </p>

  <% unless @writable %>
    <div role="alert"
         class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-900 dark:bg-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
      <p class="font-medium">This cannot be saved yet</p>
      <p class="mt-1"><%= @not_writable_reason %></p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <form action="<%= sparrow_ui.hub_path %>" method="post" autocomplete="off" class="mt-4">
    <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="patch" autocomplete="off">
    <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>" autocomplete="off">

    <label for="sparrowkit_app_url" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
      Address<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if @app_url.present? %>
    </label>
    <input type="url"
           id="sparrowkit_app_url"
           name="app_url"
           value="<%= @app_url %>"
           placeholder="https://example.com"
           autocomplete="off"
           spellcheck="false"
           <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
           class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      The whole address, starting with
      <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">https://</code>.
      In development that is your local one, port and all:
      <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">http://localhost:3000</code>.
    </p>

    <% if @passkey_domain_inherited %>
      <%#
        The one consequence of editing this box that editing it back does not
        undo. A passkey is bound to a domain permanently and cannot be moved to
        another, so this belongs on the page where the change is made rather
        than only on the Authentication panel somebody is not looking at.

        Shown only while Authentication is inheriting this. Set a different
        passkey domain there and the two are decoupled on purpose, and this
        would be telling somebody something untrue.
      %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
        Passkeys are bound to this domain, and changing it asks everybody with a passkey to enrol
        again — a passkey cannot be moved from one domain to another. To use a different domain for
        passkeys than for the site, say so on the
        <a href="<%= sparrow_ui.root_path %>auth" class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">Authentication</a>
        page.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <label for="sparrowkit_app_name" class="mt-5 block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
      Name<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if @app_name.present? %>
    </label>
    <input type="text"
           id="sparrowkit_app_name"
           name="app_name"
           value="<%= @app_name %>"
           placeholder="Acme Corp"
           autocomplete="off"
           <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
           class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-900 disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      What this product is called, as the people using it would say it — not a domain. They see it
      when their phone or laptop asks them to sign in, and it is the name email can come from.
    </p>

    <button type="submit"
            <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
            class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-purple-900 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-purple-800 disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-50 dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950 dark:hover:bg-purple-200">
      Save
    </button>
  </form>
</section>

<h2 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">How this fits together</h2>
<ol class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  <li class="flex gap-3">
    <span aria-hidden="true" class="mt-0.5 flex size-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-purple-900 text-xs font-semibold text-white dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950">1</span>
    <span>
      <span class="font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Configure each module below.</span>
      Every value you enter is written to your application's Rails encrypted
      credentials, so it is committed with your code and never sits in a
      <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">.env</code>
      file waiting to be pasted into a chat window.
    </span>
  </li>
  <li class="flex gap-3">
    <span aria-hidden="true" class="mt-0.5 flex size-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-purple-900 text-xs font-semibold text-white dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950">2</span>
    <span>
      <span class="font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">The engines are already mounted and their tables installed.</span>
      The installer did that, which is why this page is reachable at all. The
      tables live in your schema, which you own.
    </span>
  </li>
  <li class="flex gap-3">
    <span aria-hidden="true" class="mt-0.5 flex size-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-purple-900 text-xs font-semibold text-white dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950">3</span>
    <span>
      <span class="font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Build on top.</span>
      Each module is an ordinary Rails engine with ordinary models and helpers.
      Call them from your own controllers; nothing here asks you to write your
      application inside somebody else's framework.
    </span>
  </li>
</ol>

<%#
  Which order to do them in.

  Ordered from Console.panels rather than written out here, so a module added
  later appears without this page being edited, and so the sequence on this
  page can never disagree with the order of the cards below it or the links in
  the header. Each module says where it belongs with `setup_order`.

  The "why" lines are keyed by panel so they read as sentences rather than as a
  generic "set this up" repeated three times. A module sparrow_ui has never
  heard of still gets a row, without one.
%>
<h2 class="mt-10 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Start here</h2>
<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  You can fill these in in any order, but this one saves the most backtracking.
</p>

<% why = {
     mail: "Do this one first. Everything else leans on it — an application cannot email somebody a sign-in code until mail actually sends, so if you set up accounts first you get as far as a form that looks like it worked and then nothing arrives.",
     auth: "Then accounts: how people sign in, and the address passkeys are tied to. Test it by signing yourself in, which needs mail working.",
     pay: "Last, and only if you charge for something. An application that does not take money never needs this page."
   } %>

<ol class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  <% @panels.each_with_index do |panel, index| %>
    <li class="flex gap-3">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="mt-0.5 flex size-5 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-purple-900 text-xs font-semibold text-white dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950"><%= index + 1 %></span>
      <span>
        <a href="<%= "#{request.script_name}#{panel.path}" %>"
           class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300"><%= panel.name %></a>
        <% if why[panel.key] %>
          &mdash; <%= why[panel.key] %>
        <% elsif panel.summary.present? %>
          &mdash; <%= panel.summary %>.
        <% end %>
      </span>
    </li>
  <% end %>
</ol>

<h2 class="mt-10 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Modules</h2>

<% if @panels.empty? %>
  <div class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-dashed border-slate-300 px-6 py-12 text-center dark:border-slate-700">
    <p class="text-base font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">No modules have registered a panel</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-300">
      A module appears here once it is in your Gemfile and registers itself with the console.
    </p>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
    <% @panels.each do |panel| %>
      <% status = panel.status %>
      <li>
        <a href="<%= "#{request.script_name}#{panel.path}" %>"
           class="group flex h-full flex-col rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white px-5 py-4 transition-colors hover:border-purple-900 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 dark:hover:border-purple-300">
          <span class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
            <span class="text-base font-semibold text-purple-900 dark:text-purple-300"><%= panel.name %></span>
            <%= render "sparrow_ui/console/hub/status", status: status %>
          </span>
          <% if panel.summary %>
            <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-300"><%= panel.summary %></span>
          <% end %>
          <% if status.detail %>
            <span class="mt-3 block text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300"><%= status.detail %></span>
          <% end %>
          <span class="mt-auto pt-3 text-sm font-medium text-slate-500 group-hover:text-purple-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:group-hover:text-purple-300">
            Configure <span aria-hidden="true" class="inline-block transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-0.5">&rarr;</span>
          </span>
        </a>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
<% end %>

<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/hub/implement" %>

<h2 class="mt-10 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Not configured here</h2>
<ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">Anything already in an initializer</span> — the install generator
    writes <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">config/initializers/</code>
    with comments explaining each choice. A setting that lived in two places
    would eventually disagree with itself, so it lives in one.
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">Anything that is your application's policy</span> — who may do what,
    what a plan includes, how an account is provisioned. Those are decisions you
    write, not boxes to fill in.
  </li>
</ul>

<p class="mt-8 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  Full documentation is at
  <a href="<%= "#{SparrowUi::Console::DOCS_URL}/" %>" class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">sparrowkit.co/docs</a>.
</p>

<% if @install_log %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
    The install prints very little on purpose. Everything it did &mdash; every file
    written, every route, every migration &mdash; is in
    <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800"><%= @install_log %></code>.
  </p>
<% end %>

<p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-300">
  This page is served only in development, and only to requests arriving from
  this machine. It is not reachable in production.
</p>
